## Self-games, entry ply and scaled plateau (2026-11-10 ~22:15-22:50)

- How self-games are played: no new binary. The tournament binary's `replay-games` replays any game of a
  tournament exactly (the same players, policy, temperature and move choice, seeded derive_seed(seed, t, g)), and
  checked against tournament games byte for byte it reproduces them. An entry plays itself as a pair with a twin,
  `<entry>.twin`, the same depth and spec under another name, in a players list of the entries and then the twins;
  replay-games runs with `--seed` the league's seed + 1000003 (no shared seed stream with the round-robins) and the
  tournament's policy and temperature flags. Its output games are renamed `<entry>~<entry>` in selfpool/ and
  training links them with the round-robins' qualifying games, sorted by round and names.
- Fairness: the self-play budget was the round-robins' qualifying price only, so self-games had to be added: their
  price joins `data_price_target_s` in the self-games phase (before training and self-play), and with it the
  self-play budgets, the global carry and the copy step's target. The selftest's self-games test shows cycle 1's
  budgets higher, by exactly the self-games' price, than in the same experiment without them, with the same games,
  ratings and decisions.
- Exclusion: self-games never enter tour/games.csv, so the ratings, the champion test, the head-to-head tables, the
  plateau rule, and every tool that reads tour/ (the dashboard; models/from-scratch/analysis-2026-11-10's curtab,
  curcur, champtab, xtab and plyfid) never see them. self_games.csv records them; the dashboard adds their process
  CPU to a league's CPU (its tooltip names it) and labels a ply-1 experiment's columns and tips `ply 1`.
- The analysis tools curtab, curcur, champtab and xtab now take the entries' suffix from config.json's
  `entry_ply` (the same output as before on ply-2 experiments); plyfid needs both plies and prints nothing for a
  single-ply experiment.
